Subject: The Importance of Reading

J.>(I also listen to Talk Of The Nation and several hours of local 
J.>college radio daily, CDs, tapes, a few regular TV shows a week, CNN,
J.>other cable offerings, 3-4 movies a week, and 1-2 concerts/shows a
J.>month. And I spend at least a total of an hour a day on the phone with
J.>friends and business contacts, and at least that much time in person
J.>with friends/clients/etc.

J.> - jeff

The baud rate of such exchanges is much lower than reading, however.  
There is (as yet) no substitute for writing if you want to produce 
complex, detailed, and abstract communications.  One article on GATT in 
the Economist contains *much* more information on (and understanding of) 
GATT than watching CNN and listening to National Commie Radio full time 
for two years.

This is because writing is *coding*.  The boiled down and compressed 
product of one or more human minds thinking over the course of 
minutes, months, or years.  Just as ascii text occupies less disk space 
than photos, writing is richer in *different* data than visuals.  Audio 
also tends to be serial rather than random access (like writing) so it's 
harder to scan.  

The *idea* density of audio and video is low.  Video is good for showing 
surface appearances of things but lousy at analysis.  It can't crack the 
surface.  Audio is ephemeral.  It is hard to hold in one place for 
consideration.

I am frequently asked asked how I know so much and the answer is always 
the same.  I read it.

Duncan Frissell

