From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Human Language (was The Importance of Reading)

Perhaps "degrees of abstraction" was a poor choice of phrase, but I was 
thinking about hierogliphics, not Chinese. 
 I think that written Chinese is a combination of representative symbols, 
abstract symbols, idea symbols, and phonetics. You might say that it 
embodies its own evolutionary stages.
   One important difference between Chinese and English is that our written
language is very close to our spoken language, although it does not contain
tonal information. Losing this information is a large part of the cause of
many net flame wars, as someone here just pointed out; use of "emoticons"
or smileys is a first cut at adding these intonations back into electronic
communications. Same goes for pauses, emphasis, etc.
  Chinese, on the other hand, has a single written language shared by at
least 30 spoken dialects, some of which are very different from each other.
I wonder how they avoid a "loss of syncronization," meaning that the same
symbol might mean different things to speakers of different dialects? 
   Chinese also can have five words for each symbol, distinguished by
context in writing and tone in speaking. I've seen Chinese e-mail users
number these values to avoid confusion ( I think this is something that
Taiwanese people do but am not sure)

  (Chinese and Japanese pose all sorts of interesting problems for people
developing or porting computer applications...)






