(I'm not entirely sure why this one pushed my buttons, but anyhow:)

As a fairly recent father and close observer (victim?) of postpartum depression
in real life, I'd state it somewhat more strongly: the "misguided birthing
practices" theory is pure bunkum.  I don't know if it has to do with endorphins
per se but it's certainly a chemical phenomenon.  Pregnancy and its sudden end
shove the mother's endocrine system way out of equilibrium.  It can take months
to settle back to a stable state (and not necessarily the state it started in,
as Janice and I have learned).

I'm getting a little tired of the romanticization of pregnancy and childbirth
that I keep hearing from both ecologically correct newagers as well as isn't-
the-statist-medical-establishment-awful folks, not that I have any knowledge
that David Whosefullattributionwaslost is a member of either group (though of
course more of the latter than the former are likely to be found on this list!)

The selective pressures on the evolution of pregnancy and the birthing process
basically come down to a single principal: most of the time don't kill the
mother.  And nature pushes about as close to the edge as it can get.

Janice told me of a time she drove through New England.  She kept seeing lots
of picturesque little colonial-era cemeteries in the small towns she passed
through.  This piqued her curiosity so she stopped to look at a couple of them.
She found them to be filled almost entirely with the graves of women in their
late teens and early twenties, cause of death not stated but readily inferred.
Very unsettling.

The human reproductive aparatus is a dangerous kludge.  I am looking forward to
a technological fix.  Until then I'd prefer not to hear any more blather about
the wonders of childbirth or how "natural" it is (as if this were some
intrinsically positive attribute).

