Tim Starr wrote:

> Tim May posted suggestions for stocking up on ammo in the event
> Moynihan's ammo tax gets passed.  Seems to me like what will
> really happen is that handloading will skyrocket; if you don't
> reload now, you will then.

Most people won't. A few will. A reloading press and minimal supplies
costs a few hundred bucks, enough for a few thousand rounds of ammo,
and most people would be better off buying the ammo.

(I bought an RCBS press when I first started shooting, in the mid-70s,
but I haven't used it for many years....)

Reloading becomes a major win when target shooters are firing
thousands of rounds per week (yes, this happens). And certain calibers
are better suited for reloading than others. Reloading works best with
revolvers, or pistols that are tolerant of lead cast bullets, such as
.45s. It's possible to buy jacketed bullets, hollowpoint bullets,
etc., but then the bullets become the rate-limiting component. Those
with 9mms will not likely do much shooting of cast bullets.

(Primers are the main limit, but these can be bought in large quantities.)

(Restrictions on ammo and handguns are also, I think, likely to cause
most target ranges to shut down. Thus, I expect a thousand rounds to
be enough for adequate target practice. 2000 would be better.)

> Me, I've been wondering if Nevada has a waiting period or registration.

Not now, but it soon will. A friend of mine wanted me to go with him
last weekend up to Reno to make a last purchase of guns with no paper
trail (there was a large gun show in Reno scheduled). I declined.

Anyway, once the Brady Bill takes effect (Jan. 1? later? soon), all
states will have to enforce the 5-day wait and the concomitant
paperwork that goes with it.

On the other hand, false ID is fairly cheap. 

