From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: of Newtons and Nintendoes

Tim May:

> Getting the channel info is not easy, though.

True, it would require an ongoing effort, but I imagine that TV
stations are happy to give information out about their upcoming
programming.  I also imagine that the VCR+ codes are rather
simple to figure out (it has to be a fairly simple coding
scheme and given a bunch of examples shouldn't be very difficult
for a clever person [or a GP engine] to nail down).

> But I expect relatively few users will leave their Newtons laying
> around to program their VCRs while they're gone, especially since
> dedicated units to do this are avialable already (cheaply, too).

Well, you wouldn't have to; just have it program the VCR when you're
there.  As to dedicated units, they're not as flexible as the Newt
could conceivably be; and upgrading to the "mindless drivel superhighway"
that's coming would just be a software issue.  Just think -- click
on a button to automatically build that archive of all the Three's
Company episodes!

My reasons for mentioning this were twofold: first, as a gentle joke
that the Newt should really fit into common lifestyles and to do that
it has to be TV-related, and second to start to get a clearer
picture of what Newt-technology will evolve into as apps get built.
I don't know what apps are out there or under development (I don't
really need one right now as I personally sit in front of my desktop
machine about 12 hours a day anyway).  Other apps that seem like
good ideas:

* What did I do today?  A convenient way to help with time management
  by recording how time was actually spent.  Similar DayTimer-style
  apps are of course obvious.

* Maps and navigation

* Better's apprentice; sports info for the gambler, perhaps even with
  regression programs built in (don't go to the track without it!)

* Shopping list manager.  Whenever you notice something you need to buy,
  enter it.  Then when at the store just zip out that Newt and never
  forget to buy mung beans again!

* Phone book w phone tone generator.

* Where is that thing?  Enter locations of interesting things and never
  forget where you saw something.

* Eliza, for therapy on the bus.

* Games, games, games.

* Drawing program for overhead slides and the like; work on presentations
  while stuck in traffic.

* Local event calendar.  Some enterprising local homeless person gets the
  papers and enters concerts, lectures, etc for downloading by busy
  yuppies.

* Workout apprentice (and other stopwatch functions).  Time rowing machine
  regimens, enter weight values for various nautilus exercises, keep
  track of weight.

* Calorie counter.

* Baby data -- enter everything that Baby does, for future BlackNet sale.

* Car data -- gas mileage, oil change points, etc.

* Calendar apps of various kinds.

* Scanner attachment, OCR in the library.

* Engineering formulae on-line.

* Bible verses!

* Speech rehearsing, other memorization needs.

* Speed reading trainer.

* Molecular nanotechnology simulations; protein folding.

* All the lyrics to Rush songs at your fingertips.  More generally, a
  lyrics database so you can search for a phrase from a song on the
  radio and get the artist/album!

* Conspiracy theory manager (probably wants a hypertext interface) so
  work can continue at the coffee shop, where the best thinking gets
  done anyway.

* Does it have a stapler attachment yet?

* Bartender's guide.

* Cook's helper -- recipes and timed execution steps.  Include a
  configurable stovetop CAD system / scheduler.

And so on ad nauseam!

derek


