From: mgix@jpn.thomson-di.fr (Emmanuel Mogenet)
Subject: Human Language

Elizabeth Schwartz writes:
>
>I wonder how they avoid a "loss of syncronization," meaning that the same
>symbol might mean different things to speakers of different dialects? 

They don't entirely manage to. Hong-Kong Chinese use for example, ideograms
that are nowhere else to be seen.

Another thing that has been hinted by many a Chinese Culture specialist is that
given the diversity of races, languages and religions China is made of, the
written language may well be the hidden thread that somehow binds the whole
thing together. Hence its survival.

And finally, for some more nitpicking ;), the same symbol _means_ most of the
time the same thing.  It just sounds and is used differently. That's the reason
why a even Chinese and a Japanese might be able to communicate via written
language.

